But you keep operating under the presumption that running the PR means
that you're going to die in the early game.

Running an unrestricted early game creates pressure on the player. That
pressure leads to mistakes. Those mistakes leads to deaths. It happens
because the RNG is programmed to keep game balance in an unrestricted
game. I agree that running a PR game shifts the balance point heavily in
favor of the pet. But the pet is a rough, tough killing machine.
Truthfully the pet is the most overbalanced (in the player's favor) 
element in the vanilla game. It would be perfect if it didn't stop to
eat.

Then there is the central issue of the divine AC. The counterargument I
keep getting is that it doesn't matter when you get it. But it does
because of the balance points above. Players need AC, XL slots, HP, PP,
and intrinsics/resistances to be successful in the game. XLs are
convenient because they bump a big list of these items. In fact it's
everything on the above list except for AC. But the pressure the RNG
creates from bumping XL requires speed and AC (along with HP) to manage.
Without crossing the line into what David calls "triple accounting"
divine AC changes the balance point of AC between protection and weight
(and therefore speed). In short you can get an equivalent AC without
having to carry weight to do it. And that's not triple accounting.

Since bumping the XL causes pressure, and it's a pressure that you need speed
and AC to counterbalance, then getting divine AC before bumping the XL
means that the tools necessary to counterbalance are in place before the
bump occurs.

